  "worked_solution": "Let $f(t)=\\frac{11}{6}t$ be the rate at which the gas pump dispenses gas, and let $A(T)$ be the total number of liters in the car after $T$ seconds.\n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nA(T)=A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(T) & =A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =0+{\\int }_{0}^{T}\\frac{11}{6}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(\\frac{11}{6}\\cdot \\frac{{t}^{2}}{2})|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{11{T}^{2}}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable $t.$ We have\n\n$$\nA(t)=\\frac{11{t}^{2}}{12}.\n$$",
